    ```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    Utils testUtils = new Utils();

    String path = "test/path";
    System.out.println(testUtils.vfstoURI(path));

    String editor = "editor";
    System.out.println(editorToProjectFolderUri(editor));

    String folderPath = "/test/path";
    Set<Project> projects = testUtils.findProjectsFor("/test/path", "/other/test/path");
    for (Project project : projects) {
        System.out.println(project.path);
    }
}
```
    This method demonstrates how to use the `vfstoURI`, `editorToProjectFolderUri`, and `findProjectsFor` methods in the `main` function.

**Usage Examples**

1.  Convert a file path to a URI string:
    ```java
String uri = Utils.vfstoURI("test/file.txt");
System.out.println(uri); // Output: https://example.com/file/test/file.txt
```
2.  Convert an editor name to a project folder URI string:
    ```java
String editorUri = Utils.editorToProjectFolderUri("editor");
System.out.println(editorUri); // Output: https://example.com/file/editor
```
3.  Find all projects that match the given file paths:
    ```java
Set<Project> projects = Utils.findProjectsFor("/test/path", "/other/test/path");
for (Project project : projects) {
    System.out.println(project.path);
}
// Output:
// test/path
// other/test/path
```
4.  Find all projects that match the given file paths, using the editor name as an argument:
    ```java
Set<Project> editors = Utils.findProjectsFor("editor");
for (Project project : editors) {
    System.out.println(project.path);
}
// Output:
// test/path
// other/test/path
```
